Proof RIX Phase I
Canonical Metric Definitions
Version 1.0 (Locked)
Purpose

This document defines the canonical metrics used throughout:

Proof RIX Phase I,
Milestones 2–10,
and the public reproducibility framework.

These metrics were designed to:

- quantify recursive transport organization,
- measure transfer stability,
- characterize fragmentation behavior,
- map topology-defined transport regimes.

The metric layer intentionally emphasizes:

- interpretability,
- transport behavior,
- recursive organization,
- topology structure

rather than:

opaque embedding-space similarity alone.
Canonical Metric Layer

Proof RIX Phase I consolidates the framework into four primary metrics:

Metric	Purpose
Recursive Capacity Efficiency (RCE)	transfer retained per recursive capacity
Transport Robustness Score (TRS)	perturbation stability
Bottleneck Dominance (BD)	transport concentration stress
Phase Stability Index (PSI)	distance from fragmentation boundaries

These metrics collectively define:

recursive transport topology behavior.
1. Recursive Capacity Efficiency (RCE)
Definition

Recursive Capacity Efficiency measures:

how efficiently meaningful transport organization is preserved under recursive compression.

RCE captures:

transport retained,
transfer retained,
and stabilization retained

per unit recursive representational capacity.

Intuition

High RCE systems:

preserve transfer under aggressive compression,
maintain recursive organization efficiently,
and exhibit compact transport structure.

Low RCE systems:

require inflated recursive capacity,
fragment under compression,
and lose transport continuity rapidly.
