Recursive Transfer Capacity Threshold (RTCT)
Definition

RTCT is defined as:

the minimum recursive representational capacity required to achieve a specified transfer threshold.

Example Thresholds
Threshold	Interpretation
ρ ≥ 0.05	weak transfer
ρ ≥ 0.10	meaningful transfer
ρ ≥ 0.15	strong transfer
Interpretation

Lower RTCT:

more compact transport geometry,
stronger recursive efficiency,
and greater transfer compatibility.

Higher RTCT:

recursive capacity inflation,
fragmentation-sensitive transport,
and weak stabilization.
Metric Philosophy

The Proof RIX metric layer is designed to measure:

transport organization rather than static similarity.

The framework prioritizes:

valid transformations,
recursive organization,
admissibility structure,
rerouting behavior,
and multiscale transport coherence.

This differs fundamentally from:

unconstrained embedding similarity,
purely feature-space distances,
or static coordinate organization.
Canonical Interpretation Hierarchy

The recommended interpretation order is:

